      "proposed_laureates": ["Hidetoshi Katori", "Jun Ye"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Optical lattice clocks turned atomic spectroscopy into an exceptionally precise and stable measurement platform, with significance for fundamental physics as well as metrology.",
        "attribution": "Katori and Ye made distinct, influential contributions to the clock concepts and their experimental realization.",
        "maturity": "The clocks have been independently developed and improved over many years; their precision and reproducibility are well established.",
        "uncertainties": ["The extent to which the eventual prize should include later clock-development contributions is difficult to delimit."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 2,
      "ballot_id": "B026",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Immanuel Bloch", "Peter Zoller", "Tilman Esslinger"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Ultracold atoms in optical lattices made controlled studies of Hubbard-model physics possible and brought quantum many-body questions into an unusually direct experimental setting.",
        "attribution": "Bloch, Esslinger, and Zoller represent complementary experimental and theoretical advances, though the broader field has many essential contributors.",
        "maturity": "The platform and its scientific results have been tested and extended across laboratories over a substantial period.",
        "uncertainties": ["It is not clear whether the central achievement is best defined as quantum simulation, particular many-body discoveries, or the enabling experimental platform."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 3,
      "ballot_id": "B001",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Antoine Browaeys", "Mikhail Lukin"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Rydberg blockade and programmable neutral-atom arrays opened a powerful route to preparing and studying interacting quantum matter with control over individual particles.",
        "attribution": "Browaeys and Lukin are prominent experimental leaders; the theory and development of the underlying methods involve a wider community.",
        "maturity": "The platform has produced striking results and rapid progress, but its long-term scientific reach is still unfolding.",
        "uncertainties": ["The field is young, and it remains uncertain which discoveries will prove most durable beyond the platform's early demonstrations."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B051",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Michael Berry", "Yakir Aharonov"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov–Bohm and Berry phases revealed that quantum phase has observable consequences and reshaped understanding across atomic, condensed-matter, and other areas of physics.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov and Berry are central to the two named conceptual advances, although experimental demonstrations and later applications involved many researchers.",
        "maturity": "These are foundational ideas, confirmed and used extensively over decades.",
        "uncertainties": ["The candidate combines two related but distinct contributions, making the scope of a single prize difficult to define."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B007",
      "proposed_laureates": ["David E. Pritchard", "Mark A. Kasevich"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Atom interferometry brought coherent matter waves to precision measurement and enabled sensitive tests of inertial and gravitational effects.",
        "attribution": "Pritchard and Kasevich represent important foundations and experimental development, but the technique was built by several groups.",
        "maturity": "The method has a long record of increasingly precise experiments and broad independent use.",
        "uncertainties": ["It is challenging to separate a single discovery from the continuing development of a family of instruments and applications."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 6,
      "ballot_id": "B009",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Carlton M. Caves", "H. Jeff Kimble", "Nergis Mavalvala"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Squeezed light established a way to reduce quantum measurement noise and has become important for precision interferometry, including gravitational-wave detection.",
        "attribution": "Caves supplied key theoretical insight, while Kimble and Mavalvala represent experimental development and realization in precision interferometry.",
        "maturity": "The principle is well established and has demonstrated practical value in operating instruments.",
        "uncertainties": ["The credit spans theory, optical experiments, and later large-scale implementation; the proper boundary of the contribution is unsettled."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 7,
      "ballot_id": "B008",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Charles L. Kane", "Eugene J. Mele", "Laurens W. Molenkamp"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The quantum spin Hall effect and topological insulators revealed a new organizing principle for electronic phases, with robust boundary states and broad conceptual influence.",
        "attribution": "Kane and Mele developed the theoretical prediction, while Molenkamp's experiments established key realizations.",
        "maturity": "The basic phenomena have been observed and studied in multiple material systems, and the conceptual framework is firmly established.",
        "uncertainties": ["The candidate spans theory and multiple experimental realizations; the distinctive contribution of each credited person needs careful delimitation."]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 8,
      "ballot_id": "B003",
      "proposed_laureates": ["Daniel J. Eisenstein", "John A. Peacock", "Shaun Cole"],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The detection of baryon acoustic oscillations in galaxy clustering provided a robust cosmic distance scale and a powerful tool for measuring the expansion history of the Universe.",
        "attribution": "Eisenstein, Peacock, and Cole are closely associated with the prediction, detection, and interpretation of the signal, though the surveys were collective efforts.",
        "maturity": "The feature has since been measured across surveys and redshifts, giving the result strong independent confirmation.",
        "uncertainties": ["The division of credit between theoretical groundwork, the first clear detection, and subsequent survey leadership is not straightforward."]
      }
    }
  ]
